# Flutter Hot Reload Rule

Sync note: this rule must stay semantically identical across Claude rules, Copilot instructions, and Cursor rules; update all three files in the same change.

After editing a `.dart` file under `lib/`, push the change into any running Dart or Flutter app through the Dart MCP server, so the running app reflects the code on disk.

## Precondition

This rule acts only when the Dart MCP server (`dart mcp-server`) is connected in the current session. When it is not connected, say once per session that hot reload is skipped because the server is unavailable, and otherwise do nothing. Never claim a reload or restart that did not run.

## When to skip

- **Files outside `lib/`**: do not trigger for edits under `test/`, `integration_test/`, `benchmark/`, `test_driver/`, `example/`, or anywhere else outside `lib/`.
- **Comments and documentation only**: do not trigger when the change touches only comments, doc comments, or whitespace.
- **No running app**: do not launch an app to satisfy this rule. Discovery that finds no running instance ends the rule.

## Discover and connect

Find running app instances with the Dart MCP server's discovery tools (`dtd`, `list_running_apps`, or `vm_service`).

## Reload or restart

- Run `hot_reload` after changes to UI widgets, including `build` methods of stateful widgets, or to simple method bodies.
- Run `hot_restart` when the change touches `main()`, state initialization such as `initState`, global or static state, or other fundamental logic that a reload does not re-run. A restart discards in-memory app state; when the user is mid-debug-session, say a restart is needed and ask before running it.
- Report which one ran and its result. A reload that reports errors is a failed check, not a completed one.

